#import "main-defs.typ": *
#import "statements.typ": *

== Finite residue class field case <sec:finite-residue-class-field>

Throughout this section we assume that $overline(R)$ is a finite field with $q$
elements. Let $D$ be a skewfield with center $K$, and let $(D : K) = n^2$. We
call $n$ the _index_#idx("index (of skewfield)") of $D$. Denote by $v$ the
valuation $v_K$ on $K$, and by $v_D$ that on $D$, as in
@eq:normalized-skewfield-valuation. Let $Delta$ be the unique maximal $R$-order
in $D$. We shall see here that the structures of $D$ and $Delta$ can be
described explicitly in this case, and depend only on the index $n$ and some
integer $r$ such that $1 <= r <= n$, $(r, n) = 1$.

The discussion to follow depends heavily on the results listed in
§~@ss:extensions-of-complete-fields, and we shall use the notation introduced
there. Since $overline(R)$ is finite, we know from @th:unramified-finite-residue
and @cor:frobenius that for each positive integer $f$, there is a unique
unramified extension $W$ of $K$ with $(W : K) = f$, given by $W = K(omega)$,
where $omega$ is a primitive $(q^f - 1)$th root of 1. If $o_W$ denotes the
valuation ring of $W$, and $overline(o)_W$ the residue class field of $o_W$,
then
$
  o_W = R[omega], quad overline(o)_W = overline(R)[overline(omega)], quad
  (W : K) = (overline(o)_W : overline(R)) = f.
$
Further, $W slash K$ is a galois extension with cyclic galois group of order
$f$, generated by the Frobenius automorphism $sigma$ defined by
$omega -> omega^q$. Likewise, $overline(o)_W slash overline(R)$ is a galois
extension, with galois group cyclic of order $f$, generated by the automorphism
$overline(sigma)$ which maps $overline(omega)$ onto $overline(omega)^q$.

Before turning to the study of skewfields, we need one more preliminary result.

#theorem[
  Let $W$ be an unramified extension of $K$ of degree $f$, and let $v$ be the
  valuation on $K$. Given any element $alpha in K$, the equation
  $
    N_(W slash K) x = alpha, quad x in W,
  $
  is solvable for $x$ if and only if $f$ divides $v(alpha)$.
] <th:unramified-norm-equation>

#proof[
  The prime element $pi$ of $R$ is also a prime element for $o_W$. Each nonzero
  $x in W$ is thus expressible as $x = pi^k x_0$, $x_0 in u(o_W)$. Then
  $v_W (x_0) = 0$, whence $N x_0 in u(R)$, where $N$ denotes $N_(W slash K)$.
  But
  $
    N x = pi^(k f) N(x_0),
  $
  and thus the condition that $f$ divides $v(alpha)$ is a necessary one.

  To prove sufficiency, it is enough to deal with the case where
  $alpha in u(R)$. Since the galois groups of $W slash K$ and of
  $overline(o)_W slash overline(R)$ are isomorphic, it follows at #source(
    153,
  )once from Exercise~@exc:galois-char-pol that
  $
    overline(N(alpha)) = overline(N) (overline(alpha)), quad
    overline(T(alpha)) = overline(T) (overline(alpha)), quad alpha in o_W,
  $
  where $overline(N)$, $overline(T)$ are the norm and trace maps from
  $overline(o)_W$ to $overline(R)$, while $N$ and $T$ are those from $W$ to $K$.
  We shall show that both $overline(N)$ and $overline(T)$ are epic. We know this
  for $overline(T)$, since $overline(o)_W$ is separable over $overline(R)$ (see
  @th:separable-trace-form). Next, the multiplicative group of $overline(o)_W$
  is cyclic of order $q^f - 1$, with generator $overline(omega)$. Since
  $Gal(overline(o)_W slash overline(R))$ is cyclic with generator
  $overline(sigma) : overline(omega) -> overline(omega)^q$, the distinct
  algebraic conjugates of $overline(omega)$ over $overline(R)$ are
  $
    {overline(omega)^(q^i) : 0 <= i <= f - 1}.
  $
  Each nonzero element of $overline(o)_W$ is of the form $overline(omega)^t$,
  $1 <= t <= q^f - 1$, and (by Exercise~@exc:galois-char-pol) we have
  $
    overline(N) (overline(omega)^t)
    = overline(omega)^(t(1 + q + dots.c + q^(f-1)))
    = overline(omega)^(t(q^f - 1) slash (q - 1)).
  $
  Thus $overline(N) (overline(omega)^t) = 1$ if and only if $(q - 1) | t$. Hence
  exactly $(q^f - 1) slash (q - 1)$ elements of $overline(o)_W$ have norm 1, so
  there are $q - 1$ distinct nonzero norms. This proves that $overline(N)$ is
  epic.

  Continuing with the proof, let $alpha in u(R)$. Since $overline(N)$ is epic,
  we may choose $b_0 in o_W$ such that $alpha equiv N b_0 mod pi R$. For
  $i >= 1$, suppose that we have found an element
  $
    a_i = b_0 + pi b_1 + dots.c + pi^(i-1) b_(i-1) in o_W
  $
  such that
  $
    alpha equiv N a_i (mod pi^i R).
  $
  Of course $a_i in u(o_W)$ since $N a_i in u(R)$. We proceed to construct the
  next approximation, and set $a_(i+1) = a_i + b pi^i$, with $b in o_W$ to be
  determined so that
  $
    alpha equiv N a_(i+1) (mod pi^(i+1) R).
  $ <eq:norm-approximation>
  Since $i >= 1$, by Exercise~@exc:galois-char-pol we have
  $
    N a_(i+1) & = product_(s=0)^(f-1) (sigma^s (a_i) + sigma^s (b) pi^i) \
              & equiv N a_i + pi^i sum_(s=0)^(f-1) c_s (mod pi^(i+1) R),
  $
  where for each $s$,
  $
    c_s = a_i dot sigma(a_i) dots.c sigma^(s-1) (a_i) dot sigma^(s+1) (a_i)
    dots.c sigma^(f-1) (a_i) dot sigma^s (b).
  $
  If we set $y = sigma(a_i) dot sigma^2 (a_i) dots.c sigma^(f-1) (a_i)$, then
  $c_s = sigma^s (y) dot sigma^s (b) = sigma^s (y b)$, whence
  $
    sum_(s=0)^(f-1) c_s = T_(W slash K) (y b).
  $
  #source(154)Condition @eq:norm-approximation thus becomes
  $
    alpha equiv N a_i + pi^i T_(W slash K) (y b) (mod pi^(i+1) R).
  $
  But $y$ is a unit in $o_W$, and $overline(T)$ is epic, so we can always choose
  $b in o_W$ satisfying the above condition. We have therefore obtained our next
  approximation $a_(i+1)$. If we set $x = lim_(i -> infinity) a_i$, then
  $x in o_W$ and $alpha = N_(W slash K) x$, which completes the proof of the
  theorem.
]

Now we are ready to consider skewfields. The first important consequence of the
hypothesis that $overline(R)$ be finite is as follows:

#theorem(major: true)[
  If $(D : K) = n^2$, then
  $
    e(D slash K) = f(D slash K) = n.
  $
] <th:local-skewfield-e-f>

#proof[
  $overline(Delta)$ is a skewfield of finite dimension $f$ over $overline(R)$,
  hence is a finite skewfield. By Wedderburn's Theorem @th:finite-skewfield,
  $overline(Delta)$ must be a field. Hence
  $overline(Delta) = overline(R)(overline(a))$ for some $a in Delta$, so by
  @th:skewfield-ef or @th:extensions-complete-fields we have
  $
    (K(a) : K) >= (overline(Delta) : overline(R)) = f.
  $
  But $K(a)$ is a subfield of $D$, whence $(K(a) : K) <= n$ by
  @th:maximal-subfields. Therefore $f <= n$, so $f = n$ by
  @th:skewfield-e-divides-index. Finally, $e f = n^2$ implies that $e = n$, and
  the proof is complete.
]

As a consequence of the above theorem, we now know that
$
  pi Delta = frak(p)^n, quad overline(Delta) = overline(R)(xi),
$
where $frak(p)$ is the unique maximal ideal of $Delta$, $pi$ is a prime element
of $R$, and $xi$ is a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1 over $overline(R)$. We
wish to prove, in analogy with the results of
§~@ss:extensions-of-complete-fields for the case of fields, that the skewfield
$D$ comes from an unramified extension, followed by a completely ramified
extension. To begin with, choose any element $omega in Delta$ such that
$overline(omega) = xi$, and set $W = K(omega)$. Then $W$ is a subfield of $D$,
and by @th:unramified-extensions $W$ is an unramified extension of $K$ such that
$(W : K) = (overline(Delta) : overline(R)) = n$, and hence $W$ is a maximal
subfield of $D$. Further, by @th:unramified-finite-residue $W$ is a cyclotomic
extension of $K$ containing all of the $(q^n - 1)$th roots of 1. Hence we could
have chosen $omega$ to be a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1 over $K$. We shall
call $W$ an _inertia field_ of $D$. #idx("inertia field")

We now know that every skewfield $D$ of index $n$ contains an inertia field
$K(omega)$ as maximal subfield, where $omega$ is a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root
of 1 over $K$. To what extent is the subfield $W$ of $D$ unique? It is unique up
to $K$-isomorphism by @th:unramified-finite-residue, but there may be many
mutually $K$-isomorphic subfields of $D$. Obviously $W = K(omega^j)$ for any $j$
relatively prime to $q^n - 1$, so replacing #source(155)$omega$ by such a power
$omega^j$ does not affect $W$. However, (see
Exercise~@exc:infinitely-many-zeros) there are usually more than $q^n - 1$
distinct $(q^n - 1)$th roots of 1 in $D$. Indeed, if $alpha in D$ is nonzero,
then $alpha omega alpha^(-1)$ is also a $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1, and need not be
a power of $omega$. We call the elements $omega$, $alpha omega alpha^(-1)$
_conjugate_ in $D$, and likewise the fields $W$ and $alpha W alpha^(-1)$ are
_conjugate_ subfields of $D$. #idx("conjugate")

#theorem[
  The inertia field of $D$ is unique up to conjugacy.
] <th:inertia-field-unique>

#proof[
  By @th:unramified-finite-residue, $W$ is unique up to $K$-isomorphism. Hence
  $W$ is unique up to conjugacy in $D$ by @cor:automorphisms-inner. We remark
  that if $K$ is an infinite field, then by Exercise~@exc:infinitely-many-zeros,
  there are infinitely many primitive $(q^n - 1)$th roots of 1 in $D$, and thus
  there are infinitely many distinct inertia fields in $D$.
]

Let $pi_D$ be a prime element of $Delta$. Since $pi Delta = pi_D^n Delta$, the
field $K(pi_D)$ is a completely ramified extension of $K$ of degree $n$, and is
a maximal subfield of $D$. By @th:skewfield-ef we have
$
  Delta = dsum_(i, j=0)^(n-1) R omega^i pi_D^j = R[omega, pi_D], quad
  D = K[omega, pi_D].
$
Thus $D$ is obtained by adjoining the element $pi_D$ to any of its inertia
fields $K(omega)$, or equivalently, by adjoining $omega$ to the field $K(pi_D)$.
Note that $omega$ and $pi_D$ do _not_ commute, unless $n = 1$.

The inertia field $K(omega)$ is uniquely determined up to $K$-isomorphism by the
index $n$, and our next task is to improve our choice of the prime element
$pi_D$.

#theorem[
  Let $omega in D$ be a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1, and let $pi$ be any
  prime element of $R$. Then there exists a prime element $pi_D in Delta$ such
  that
  $
    pi_D^n = pi, quad pi_D omega pi_D^(-1) = omega^(q^r),
  $
  where $r$ is a positive integer such that $1 <= r <= n$, $(r, n) = 1$. The
  integer $r$ is uniquely determined by $D$, and does not depend upon the choice
  of $omega$ or $pi$.
] <th:local-skewfield-normal-form>

#proof[
  By Exercise~@exc:frobenius-power-conjugate, there exists a nonzero
  $alpha in D$ such that
  $
    alpha omega alpha^(-1) = omega^q.
  $
  Replacing $alpha$ by $pi^l alpha$ if need be, we may assume that
  $alpha in Delta$. Now let $v_D (alpha) = k$, and put $h = n slash (k, n)$.
  Then
  $
    alpha^h = epsilon dot pi^b, quad epsilon in u(Delta), quad b in bold(Z),
  $
  and $h$ is the least positive integer for which such an expression for
  $alpha^h$ is possible. Then
  $
    omega^(q^h) = alpha^h dot omega dot alpha^(-h)
    = epsilon dot omega dot epsilon^(-1) equiv omega (mod frak(p)),
  $
  #source(156)since $overline(Delta) = Delta slash frak(p)$ is a field. But
  $overline(omega)$ is a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1 in $overline(Delta)$,
  whence $n = h$, and so $(k, n) = 1$. Now choose $r, t in bold(Z)$ with
  $k r - n t = 1$, $1 <= r <= n$, so surely $(r, n) = 1$. Setting
  $
    pi_D = pi^(-t) alpha^r,
  $
  we have
  $
    v_D (pi_D) = r v_D (alpha) - t v_D (pi) = r k - t n = 1,
  $
  so $pi_D$ is indeed a prime element of $Delta$. Furthermore,
  $
    pi_D dot omega dot pi_D^(-1) = alpha^r dot omega dot alpha^(-r)
    = omega^(q^r).
  $
  Therefore $pi_D^n$ commutes with $omega$, and certainly also commutes with
  $pi_D$. Thus $pi_D^n$ lies in the center of $D$, and is then obviously a prime
  element of $R$.

  We do not know at this point that $pi_D^n$ equals a _preassigned_ prime
  element $pi$ of $R$, and we show next that we can modify the above $pi_D$ so
  as to accomplish this. For any $lambda in u(o_W)$, $lambda pi_D$ is also a
  prime element of $Delta$, and
  $
    (lambda pi_D) dot omega dot (lambda pi_D)^(-1)
    = lambda dot omega^(q^r) dot lambda^(-1) = omega^(q^r),
  $
  since $lambda$ lies in the inertia field $K(omega)$. It remains to prove that
  $lambda$ may be chosen so that $(lambda pi_D)^n = pi$. Now the galois group
  $Gal(W slash K)$ is cyclic of order $n$, generated by the automorphism
  $sigma : omega -> omega^q$. Since $(r, n) = 1$, the automorphism
  $rho : omega -> omega^(q^r)$ also generates this galois group. But
  $rho(x) = pi_D x pi_D^(-1)$, $x in W$. Therefore by
  Exercise~@exc:galois-char-pol,
  $
    N_(W slash K) x & = x dot pi_D x pi_D^(-1) dot pi_D^2 x pi_D^(-2) dots.c
                      pi_D^(n-1) x pi_D^(-(n-1)) \
                    & = (x pi_D)^n dot pi_D^(-n), quad x in W.
  $
  This shows that for each $lambda in u(o_W)$,
  $
    (lambda pi_D)^n = (N_(W slash K) lambda) dot pi_D^n.
  $
  Now any preassigned prime element $pi$ of $R$ is expressible as
  $pi = beta dot pi_D^n$ for some $beta in u(R)$. By
  @th:unramified-norm-equation we may choose $lambda in u(o_W)$ such that
  $N_(W slash K) lambda = beta$. Therefore $(lambda pi_D)^n = pi$, as desired.

  Finally, we prove the uniqueness of $r$. To begin with, if $omega'$ is another
  primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1 in $D$, then there exists a nonzero
  $beta in D$ such that $omega' = beta omega^t beta^(-1)$ for some $t$. Let
  $pi'_D = beta pi_D beta^(-1)$, another prime element of $Delta$. Then we have
  $
    (pi'_D)^n = beta pi beta^(-1) = pi, quad
    pi'_D omega' pi'_D^(-1) = beta dot omega^(t q^r) dot beta^(-1)
    = (omega')^(q^r),
  $
  so the choice of $r$ does not depend on which root of unity is used. We must
  still show that $r$ does not depend on the choice of the prime element $pi_D$.
  Let $pi'$ be any prime element of $Delta$, and suppose that
  $
    pi' dot omega dot pi'^(-1) = omega^(q^s)
  $
  #source(157)for some $s$ between 1 and $n$. We may write $pi' = gamma pi_D$
  with $gamma in u(Delta)$, and then
  $
    omega^(q^s) = gamma pi_D dot omega dot (gamma pi_D)^(-1)
    = gamma dot omega^(q^r) dot gamma^(-1).
  $
  Passing to $overline(Delta)$ we obtain the equality
  $overline(omega)^(q^s) = overline(omega)^(q^r)$. Therefore $s = r$, since
  $overline(omega)$ is a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of unity. This completes
  the proof of the theorem.
]

The above shows that once the complete field $K$ is given, the skewfield $D$ is
completely determined by its index $n$, and by the integer $r$. Indeed, we first
form the field $K(omega)$, with $omega$ any primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1.
Then we pick any prime $pi in R$, and adjoin to the field $W$ an element $pi_D$
satisfying the conditions listed in @th:local-skewfield-normal-form. This
determines the skewfield $D = K(omega, pi_D)$ up to $K$-isomorphism. We call the
fraction $r slash n$ the _Hasse invariant_#idx("Hasse invariant")#idx(
  "invariant (Hasse)",
)#footnote[Later on, we shall call the fraction $r' slash n$ the Hasse invariant
  of $D$, where $r' in bold(Z)$ is chosen so that $r r' equiv 1 (mod n)$,
  $1 <= r' <= n$.] of $D$.

We are still left with the problem of deciding whether each fraction $r slash n$
arises from some skewfield.

#theorem[
  Let $1 <= r <= n$, $(r, n) = 1$. Given the complete field $K$, there exists a
  skewfield $D$ with center $K$, index $n$, and Hasse invariant $r slash n$.
] <th:local-skewfield-existence>

#proof[
  Let $W = K(omega)$, where $omega$ is a primitive $(q^n - 1)$th root of 1. If
  there is such a skewfield $D$ with the desired properties, then $W$ must be a
  maximal subfield of $D$, and hence $W$ splits $D$. Therefore
  $W times.o_K D tilde.equiv M_n (W)$, and hence each element $d in D$ is
  representable by a matrix $d^* in M_n (W)$. We will therefore try to find a
  set of matrices in $M_n (W)$ which constitute a skewfield having the desired
  properties.

  Let $theta$ be the automorphism of $W$ for which $theta(omega) = omega^(q^r)$,
  and let $pi$ be a prime element of $R$. For $alpha in W$, define
  $
    alpha^* = mat(
      delim: "[",
      alpha, 0, 0, dots, 0;
      0, theta(alpha), 0, dots, 0;
      0, 0, theta^2 (alpha), dots, 0;
      dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v;
      0, 0, 0, dots, theta^(n-1) (alpha);
    ), quad
    pi_D^* = mat(
      delim: "[",
      0, 1, 0, dots, 0;
      0, 0, 1, dots, 0;
      dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v;
      0, 0, 0, dots, 1;
      pi, 0, 0, dots, 0;
    ).
  $
  The map $alpha -> alpha^*$, $alpha in W$, gives a $K$-isomorphism of $W$ onto
  the field $W^* = K(omega^*) subset M_n (W)$, where we identify each
  $lambda in K$ with the scalar matrix $lambda I_n in M_n (K)$. It is easily
  checked that
  $
    (pi_D^*)^n = pi I_n, quad
    pi_D^* dot omega^* dot (pi_D^*)^(-1) = (omega^*)^(q^r).
  $
  We now set
  $
    D = K[omega^*, pi_D^*] = sum_(i, j=0)^(n-1) K (omega^*)^i (pi_D^*)^j,
  $
  #source(158)a $K$-subalgebra of $M_n (W)$. We shall verify that $D$ is the
  desired skewfield.

  Each element $a in D$ is expressible as a $K$-linear combination of the $n^2$
  elements ${(omega^*)^i dot (pi_D^*)^j}$. Since $(omega^*)^i = (omega^i)^*$, it
  follows that $a$ is expressible in the form
  $
    a = sum_(j=0)^(n-1) alpha_j^* (pi_D^*)^j, quad alpha_j in W.
  $
  Since
  $
    (pi_D^*)^j = mat(delim: "[", 0, I_(n-j); pi I_j, 0),
  $
  we obtain
  $
    a = mat(
      delim: "[",
      alpha_0, alpha_1, alpha_2, dots, alpha_(n-1);
      pi theta(alpha_(n-1)), theta(alpha_0), theta(alpha_1), dots,
      theta(alpha_(n-2));
      pi theta^2 (alpha_(n-2)), pi theta^2 (alpha_(n-1)), theta^2 (alpha_0),
      dots, theta^2 (alpha_(n-3));
      dots.v, dots.v, dots.v, dots.down, dots.v;
      pi theta^(n-2) (alpha_2), pi theta^(n-2) (alpha_3),
      pi theta^(n-2) (alpha_4), dots, theta^(n-2) (alpha_1);
      pi theta^(n-1) (alpha_1), pi theta^(n-1) (alpha_2),
      pi theta^(n-1) (alpha_3), dots, theta^(n-1) (alpha_0);
    ).
  $ <eq:cyclic-matrix-form>
  Hence if $a = 0$, then each $alpha_i = 0$. This shows that $D$ is a vector
  space over $W$ with basis ${(pi_D^*)^j : 0 <= j <= n - 1}$, and therefore
  $(D : K) = n^2$.

  Next suppose that $a in D$ is a zero divisor, but that $a != 0$. Let $a$ be
  given as above; after replacing $a$ by $pi^l a$ with suitable $l$, we may
  assume that each $alpha_j in o_W$, and that at least one $alpha_j$ is a unit
  in $o_W$. (This depends on the observation that $pi$ is also a prime element
  for $o_W$.) Now (by Exercise~@exc:galois-char-pol),
  $
    det a equiv N_(W slash K) alpha_0 quad mod pi o_W.
  $
  But $det a = 0$, since $a$ is a zero divisor in $D$. Therefore
  $N_(W slash K) alpha_0 in pi o_W inter R$, whence $alpha_0$ is not a unit in
  $o_W$. Hence we may write $alpha_0 = pi beta_0$ for some $beta_0 in o_W$. But
  then
  $
    det a equiv (-1)^(n-1) pi dot N_(W slash K) alpha_1 quad mod pi^2 o_W,
  $
  so also $alpha_1 = pi beta_1$ for some $beta_1 in o_W$. Continuing in this
  way, it follows that each $alpha_j$ is a multiple of $pi$, which is a
  contradiction. This proves that $D$ has no zero divisors except 0. Hence for
  each nonzero $a in D$, the $K$-homomorphism $x -> a x$, $x in D$, is monic.
  Since $(D : K)$ is finite, this shows that $D = a D = D a$ for each nonzero
  $a in D$, and hence $D$ is a skewfield.

  Observe next that $W^*$ is a subfield of $D$, and that $(W^* : K) = n$. If
  $a in D$ commutes with $omega^*$, then since the diagonal entries
  $omega, theta(omega), dots, theta^(n-1) (omega)$ of $omega^*$ are pairwise
  distinct, it follows that $a$ is a diagonal matrix. Hence by #source(
    159,
  )@eq:cyclic-matrix-form we have $a in W^*$, and so we have shown that $W^*$ is
  a maximal subfield of $D$. On the other hand, if $a$ also commutes with
  $pi_D^*$, then writing $a = alpha_0^*$ with $alpha_0 in W$, it follows at once
  that $theta(alpha_0) = alpha_0$. Hence $alpha_0 in K$, and $a = u I_n$ for
  some $u in K$, which shows that $K$ is the center of $D$. Finally, the
  equation $(pi_D^*)^n = pi I_n$ proves that $pi_D^*$ is a prime element for
  $D$. Thus $D$ is a skewfield with Hasse invariant $r slash n$, as claimed.
]

We shall conclude this section by calculating the discriminant
$d(Delta slash R)$ of the $R$-order $Delta$ (see
§~@sec:existence-of-maximal-orders) as well as the _different_
$frak(D) = frak(D)(Delta slash R)$, defined below. To begin with, let
$tr : D -> K$ be the reduced trace map, and let $tau : D times D -> K$ be the
trace form given by $tau(a, b) = tr(a b)$, $a, b in D$. By
@th:reduced-trace-form, $tau$ is a symmetric nondegenerate associative bilinear
form. We may then define
$
  tilde(Delta) = {x in D : tau(x, Delta) subset R}
  = {x in D : tr(x Delta) subset R}.
$
By Exercise~@exc:dual-lattice-form, $tilde(Delta)$ is a full $R$-lattice in $D$,
and is clearly a two-sided $Delta$-module. Since
$pi_D^r tilde(Delta) subset Delta$ for some $r$, it follows from
@th:skewfield-order-ideals that
$
  tilde(Delta) = pi_D^(-m) dot Delta
$
for some integer $m$, and $m >= 0$ since $tilde(Delta) supset Delta$. Call
$tilde(Delta)$ the _inverse different_#idx("inverse different"), and define the
_different_#idx("different") as
$
  frak(D) = frak(D)(Delta slash R) = pi_D^m dot Delta.
$

For a nonzero ideal $L = pi_D^l Delta$ of $Delta$, define the _norm_
#idx("norm (of ideal)")
$
  N_(D slash K) L = ord_R (Delta slash L),
$
where $ord_R$ is the $R$-order ideal (see §~@ss:ideal-theory-modules). We have,
as in @rem:ramification-comments[(iii)],
$
  ord_R (Delta slash pi_D^l Delta) = {ord_R (Delta slash pi_D Delta)}^l
  = P^(n l),
$
since $(overline(Delta) : overline(R)) = n$ in this case. Therefore
$
  N_(D slash K) (pi_D^l Delta) = (N_(D slash K) pi_D)^l dot R, quad
  N_(D slash K) frak(p) = P^n.
$

#lemma[
  $d(Delta slash R) = N_(D slash K) (frak(D)(Delta slash R))$.
] <lem:skewfield-discriminant-norm-different>

#proof[
  Keeping the above notation, we observe that left multiplication by $pi_D^m$
  gives an $R$-isomorphism
  $
    tilde(Delta) slash Delta tilde.equiv Delta slash frak(D).
  $
  Therefore
  $
    N_(D slash K) (frak(D)) = ord_R (Delta slash frak(D))
    = ord_R (tilde(Delta) slash Delta).
  $
  Now we imitate the proof of @th:discriminant-local-and-basis. We may write
  $
    Delta = dsum R x_i, quad tilde(Delta) = dsum R y_j, quad "where" quad
    tr(x_i y_j) = delta_(i j),
  $
  #source(160)and the subscripts $i, j$ range from 1 to $n^2$. Set
  $x_i = sum a_(i j) y_j$, $a_(i j) in K$. Then $a_(i j) = tr(x_i x_j)$, and so
  by Exercise~@exc:order-ideal-determinant we have
  $
    ord_R (tilde(Delta) slash Delta) = R dot det(a_(i j)) = d(Delta slash R),
  $
  as claimed.
]

Let us now calculate $frak(D)(Delta slash R)$ and $d(Delta slash R)$ explicitly.

#theorem(major: true)[
  We have
  $
    frak(D)(Delta slash R) = pi_D^(n-1) Delta, quad
    d(Delta slash R) = P^(n(n-1)).
  $
] <th:local-skewfield-different>

#proof[
  The formula for the discriminant follows from that for the different, by
  taking norms. Let us write $tilde(Delta) = pi_D^(-m) Delta$; then $m$ is
  maximal such that $tr pi_D^(-m) Delta subset R$. Hence we need only show that
  $
    tr(pi_D^(-(n-1)) Delta) subset R, quad
    tr(pi_D^(-n) Delta) subset.not R.
  $
  Suppose $pi_D$ chosen as in @th:local-skewfield-normal-form, so that
  $pi_D^n = pi$. Since
  $
    Delta = dsum_(j=0)^(n-1) o_W dot (pi_D)^j,
  $
  we have
  $
    pi_D^(-(n-1)) Delta = pi^(-1) dot pi_D Delta
    = dsum_(j=1)^(n-1) (pi^(-1) o_W) dot (pi_D)^j plus.o o_W.
  $

  Let $a in D$; to compute $tr a$, we may use the isomorphism
  $W times.o_K D tilde.equiv M_n (W)$ to represent $1 times.o a$ as an element
  of $M_n (W)$, and then take the trace of the matrix thus obtained. But the
  isomorphism $W times.o_K D tilde.equiv M_n (W)$ has been exhibited explicitly
  in @eq:cyclic-matrix-form, and hence
  $
    tr (sum_(j=0)^(n-1) alpha_j (pi_D)^j) = T_(W slash K) alpha_0, quad
    alpha_j in W.
  $
  Hence we have at once
  $
    tr(pi_D^(-(n-1)) Delta) = T_(W slash K) (o_W) subset R.
  $

  On the other hand, if $tr(pi_D^(-n) Delta) subset R$, then the above argument
  gives $T_(W slash K) (pi^(-1) o_W) subset R$, whence
  $T_(W slash K) (o_W) subset pi R$. If $overline(T)$ denotes the trace map from
  $overline(o)_W$ to $overline(R)$, then since the galois groups of $W slash K$
  and $overline(o)_W slash overline(R)$ are isomorphic, it follows that for each
  $lambda in o_W$, $overline(T) (overline(lambda))$ is the image of
  $T_(W slash K) (lambda)$. We may thus conclude that $overline(T)$ is the zero
  map, which contradicts the fact that $overline(o)_W$ is separable over
  $overline(R)$. This proves that $tr pi_D^(-n) Delta subset.not R$, and
  establishes the theorem.
]

#heading(level: 3, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Exercises]

#exercise[
  #source(161)Let $D$ be a skewfield whose center $K$ is infinite. Let
  $a in D - K$, $f(X) = minpol_K a$. Show that there are infinitely many
  elements $a' in D$ such that $f(a') = 0$.
  #hint[@exc:infinitely-many-zeros][For each nonzero $x in D$,
    $f(x a x^(-1)) = 0$. Since $a in.not K$, there exists an element $y in D$
    which does not commute with $a$; the same holds for each $y + alpha$,
    $alpha in K$. If
    $
      (y + alpha) a (y + alpha)^(-1) = (y + beta) a (y + beta)^(-1), quad
      alpha, beta in K, quad alpha != beta,
    $
    then setting $z = y + beta$, it follows that $z^(-1) (z + alpha - beta)$
    commutes with $a$. Hence $z$ commutes with $a$, a contradiction. Thus
    ${(y + alpha) a (y + alpha)^(-1) : alpha in K}$ is an infinite set of zeros
    of $f(X)$]<hint:infinitely-many-zeros>.
] <exc:infinitely-many-zeros>

#exercise[
  Keep the notation of @th:inertia-field-unique and the discussion preceding it.
  Show that for each $i$, $0 <= i <= f - 1$, $omega^(q^i)$ is conjugate to
  $omega$ in $D$.
] <exc:frobenius-power-conjugate>

#exercise[
  Let $K$ be the 2-adic completion of the rational field, $R$ its ring of 2-adic
  integers, and $v_K$ the valuation on $K$. Let
  $D = K plus.o K i plus.o K j plus.o K k$ (quaternions over $K$), and set
  $
    Lambda = R + R i + R j + R k + R a, quad a = (1 + i + j + k) slash 2.
  $
  Show how to extend $v_K$ to a valuation on $D$, and prove that $Lambda$ is its
  valuation ring. Find the discriminant of $Lambda$, and compute $e(D slash K)$,
  $f(D slash K)$, a prime element $pi_D$, the residue class field
  $overline(Delta)$, an $R$-basis for $Delta$, and the inertia fields of $D$
  over $K$.
] <exc:dyadic-quaternions>

#exercise[
  Let $K$ be the 3-adic completion of $bold(Q)$. Show that there exists an
  element $a in K$ with $a^2 = -2$. Let $Phi_8 (X) = X^4 + 1$, the cyclotomic
  polynomial of order 8. Show that
  $
    Phi_8 (X) = (X^2 - a X - 1)(X^2 + a X - 1) quad "in" quad K[X],
  $
  and that each quadratic factor is irreducible in $K[X]$. Let $omega$ be a zero
  of the first factor in some extension field of $K$. Prove that $omega$ is a
  primitive 8th root of unity, and that
  $
    X^2 - a X - 1 & = (X - omega)(X - omega^3), \
    X^2 + a X - 1 & = (X - omega^5)(X - omega^7).
  $
] <exc:three-adic-cyclotomic>

#exercise[
  Keeping the notation of @th:local-skewfield-normal-form, let $W$ be an inertia
  field for $D$. Prove that
  $
    nr_(D slash K) (pi_D) = (-1)^(n-1) pi, quad
    nr_(D slash K) (alpha) = N_(W slash K) (alpha), quad alpha in W.
  $
  #hint[@exc:reduced-norm-normal-form][Since $X^n - pi$ is irreducible in $K[X]$
    by Exercise~@exc:eisenstein-irreducible, it follows that
    $minpol_K pi_D = X^n - pi$. But then
    $
      redcharpol_(D slash K) pi_D = minpol_K pi_D,
    $
    by Exercise~@exc:reduced-char-pol-maximal-subfield. This gives the desired
    formula for $nr(pi_D)$. Next, let $alpha in W$ and let
    $f(X) = minpol_K alpha$. By Exercise~@exc:reduced-char-pol-maximal-subfield,
    $
      redcharpol_(D slash K) alpha = {f(X)}^((W : K(alpha))).
    $
    But also
    $
      charpol_(W slash K) alpha = {f(X)}^((W : K(alpha)))
    $
    #source(162)\(compare degrees!), whence
    $
      redcharpol_(D slash K) alpha = charpol_(W slash K) alpha, quad
      alpha in W.
    $
    This yields the formula for $nr(alpha)$.

    Another proof can be given by using the matrix representation of $pi_D$ and
    $alpha$ ($alpha in W$) occurring in the proof of
    @th:local-skewfield-existence. Then
    $
      nr_(D slash K) pi_D & = det pi_D^* = (-1)^(n-1) pi, \
      nr_(D slash K) alpha & = det alpha^*
      = product_(i=0)^(n-1) theta^i (alpha) = N_(W slash K) alpha.
    $] <hint:reduced-norm-normal-form>
] <exc:reduced-norm-normal-form>

#exercise[
  Let $D$ be a skewfield whose center $K$ is complete with respect to a discrete
  valuation, with a finite residue class field $overline(R)$ (as in
  §~@sec:finite-residue-class-field). Let
  $
    nr D = {nr_(D slash K) (d) : d in D}.
  $
  Prove that $nr D = K$. #hint[@exc:reduced-norm-surjective][Use the notation
    and results of the preceding exercise. Then
    $
      nr D supset N_(W slash K) (W) = {x in K : n | v_K (x)},
    $
    by @th:unramified-norm-equation. But $nr D$ also contains $(-1)^(n-1) pi$,
    whence $nr D = K$.] <hint:reduced-norm-surjective>
] <exc:reduced-norm-surjective>
